<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sighnaq</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sygnak, Suganak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sighnaq, also written Sygnak, is a ruined medieval city on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> in southern Kazakhstan. In the fourteenth century it was the capital of the White Horde, and in the fifteenth it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> khan Abu'l-Khayr. It was one of the Syr Darya towns fought over by the early Kazakh khans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a>, and later declined and was abandoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sighnaq.jpg" alt="Sighnaq" width="169" height="118" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of Kazakhstan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sighnaq.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some farming was practised in the river valleys, and the oasis towns of the middle Syr Darya, among them Sauran, <b>Sygnak</b>, Sayram and Turkistan, gave the khans access to crafts, markets and Muslim scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He conquered the steppe as far as the Syr Darya and in 1446 took <b>Sighnaq</b> and other Syr Darya towns, making them his base.</blockquote><a href="/state/uzbek-khanate">Uzbek Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The khans of the Orda line ruled nomadic Turkic and Mongol tribes in the steppes of the Syr Darya and the Irtysh, with towns such as <b>Sighnaq</b> and Sauran on the Syr Darya.</blockquote><a href="/state/white-horde">White Horde</a></li></ul></section>
